f our target  candidates as it would be in the reality  of clinical samples and so now you can  all use these kind of  primary cultures and also co-culture  them with your car t cells and really  ask yourself the question what  influences the car t-cell activity is it  only the marker expression is it a  co-expression of  immunosuppressive molecules such as pl1  and uh  actually what you see it's uh very  dependent  on the target expression of course  because for example look at this this  part two  celine here  and if i go back to the flow cytometry  you see that this cell line  only expresses cd 318 but not the other  targets and as expected also when you  look here for marker up regulation but  also cytokine release only cd 318 based  cars actually uh efficiently kill his  line up regulate uh activation markers  and also release cytokines  and for example for the aspc one  expressing all the targets you see also  killing of all the car t sets and the  same holds true for this primary derived  cell line from a primary human tumor  so with this you can then also resemble  the heterogeneity in a clinical reality  and also what you see is it's not the  only case the target expression it's  also other factors uh because also the  cell line uh activation pattern is not  always at the same level in each of  these cells  so upon then uh really nicely  characterizing your different car  constructs you of course choose the best  working ones in vitro and then you  perform in vivo studies  and here we have used um  nsg xenograph models with other aspc1 or  bxpc3 and we have injected then car t  cells and what you see here is on this  aspc one model  that cd66s can  can prolong survival of the mice but it  cannot eradicate the tumor and the same  holds true also here for the speak  species 3 model i have to say that this  is very challenging both are very  challenging models because we already  established tumors for almost two weeks  so they are solid masses before we  inject iv the car t cells you see that  tspan8  in particular with the spacer shows a  very nice response with two more  clearing after about 30 days  but only in the aspc one not in the  pxbc3 model so here you all just need  too much stabilization for tspan8 and  this again can be uh dedicated to the  target expression evaluated by flow  cytometry also xvivo uh here you see  tspan8 only very low express on the xpc3  very highly expressed on aspc one cells  and our let's say best candidate cd 318  you see very fast and efficient tumor  relocation even after less than two  weeks in both  models so of course to better understand  why a car t cell is working or not and  then we do ex vivo analysis so we reject  the the tumor or tumor remnants and then  we check this by again the maxima  technology for car t cell infiltration  tumor remnants macrophage infiltration  and target expression and this is of  course accomplished also by in that flow  cytometry analysis again to look for  potential explanations of the indivo  efficacy and for example what you can  see here is that actually cd6's z  function was not  low due to limit of target expression  because you see it's nicely expressed  even x vivo but it was a of obviously a  lag of functional car t-cell  infiltration that you see in cd 318 but  not in 66 c so you see t-cell  infiltration but not active infiltration  of these purple car t-cells here  and of course  to make the story around we also do a  lot of phenotyping on the t cells  isolated x vivo  so we dissociate different organs like  the bone marrow the spleen but also the  tumor isolate based on cd45 positive  enrichment  the car t cells and then we do  phenotyping and quantification of the  cars and their flow cytometry is of  course the method of choice because of  the very nice quantification you can do  but also we look at um  central memory versus effect of  phenotypes because this was shown to  have predictive value later on for the  clinical efficiency when you go for  translation  the last point i want to make here is  that of course this is all either flow  cytometry or 2d spatial resolution but  you might also want to go deeper in the  tissue and also see the 3d context and  that is where we use light sheet  microscopy with our ultra microscopes  which actually give you the opportunity  so in this case we did the labeling even  in vivo so we injected dyes into the  mice but you can also do it ex vivo then  you um  take out the tumor or the organ of  interest you fix it you clear it in  order to make it amendable for um  for a light sheet microscopy and now you  can really see here in green the tumor  cells in orange the vasculature and in  pink you see the cartesia infiltration  into the tumor and you can then of  course also make statistics about the  special distribution of these cells and  thereby derive much more information  about the persistence and proliferation  of the cartisates inside the tumors  so with this i want to thumb up and i  hope i could convince you that we have  provided a comprehensive workflow for  the identification of new target  candidates  here i focus today on car t-sets but  this is of course also applicable to  other cellular therapies and also  biologics that we have developed  solutions to address all the technical  challenges in the functional evaluation  of immunotherapy targets in particular  when working with primary cells  and finally that we also identified for  target candidates for cellular  immunotherapy of pancreatic cancer  showing high tumor specificity very  restricted of tumor expression and i was  not talking about this  but you can also look it up in our  publication that was recently published  in nature medicine sorry in nature  communications about the confident  bioinformatics safety level  and we were able to derive functional  car constructs in vitro and in vivo  so with this i want to thank the people  that really do the work because i'm just  sitting in the office and waiting for  nice data but this is really uh  dedicated to the people in my team uh  that compiled all the data worked on  this and also a lot of military  colleagues because it was a big  interdisciplinary project with  bioinformatics  our t-cell experts and many others  but also corporation partners in  academia and also industry and of course  also you for your attention  thank you olaf for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box located  on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started  our first question is how do you remove  the stainings between each cycle in the  maxima  yes thank you very much that's a very  good question  so we're using two different  technologies to do so  the  one and let's say that the first  technology we applied is uh  photobleaching so um we are using um  dyes that are sensitive to bleaching  which are then fine for the imaging but  after each imaging cycle we are then  stitching the dice away and we have also  proven that even if you do a couple of  hundred of stainings that is still  perfectly fine and the tissue integrity  is kept  and as a second removal  mechanism we are using enzymatic removal  of the staining and so we are using both  systems in parallel and also if you  perform one maxima run you can also use  antibody conjugates  which are of the one or the other  technology and use it on the on the very  same instrument in the same run and just  tell the instrument what removal to use  in each cycle  thank you  next question  how do you decide to culture primary  tumor cells in 2d or 3d  yeah  so  that was also let's say a surprise for  us when we started about five or six  years ago with the culturing of primary  tumor cells because the idea was that  most epithelial tumor cells at least  would grow in 2d  at least when you plate them on a  substrate where they are  happy to to adhere however what we have  seen is that in many patient samples  even epithelial tumors like pancreatic  ovarian but also colon carcinoma samples  the cells like more to grow in 3d and  tumors ferroids or organoids  and the let's say that the challenging  part is that you can not really see  before from the patient sample the  diagnosis or the stage if the cells tend  to grow into the 3d so usually what we  do is we dissociate the sample  isolate the tumor cells if needed and  then we plate them on primary surface  plates  so that promote adhesion of the cells  and we have seen that uh if the tumor  cells tend to aggregate and grow in 3d  they still do so when you have them on  these kind of plates so we do not do  initial let's say a matrix gel embedding  or so  and when we then observe during the  first passages that the cells really  like to grow better in 3d then we  usually switch to um  to different surfaces so such as ultra  low attachment plates and then we  culture them just in 3d  but yeah it is very important to really  keep it in mind that even if you culture  epithelial tumor cells and your cells  stay in the supernatant that does not  necessarily mean that they are dead but  they just  can also grow in suspension so it's  definitely good to take these suspension  cells do a live dead cell dye staining  and then check if these cells are still  viable and maybe uh just tend to to grow  so i mean there are some particular  tumors such as colorectal cancers where  there's really a huge trend to grow them  in 3d and then what you can do also  right from the beginning is to embed  them in material  and the good thing is that the media  that we developed are compatible with  these 3d material embedded  and organoculture  as well as for the 2d culture  thank you  next question  what if the enzymes used in the tumor  dissociation step cleaved potential car  targets  yeah a very important point during the  dissociation procedure that you have to  to worry about  as briefly talked about in the  presentation it's really a matter of  using  very purified or highly purified enzymes  because usually what causes the trouble  is not the the enzyme itself but the  residual activities that are still in  the enzyme due to the manufacturing  process  so most enzymes are manufactured from um  from from coot lysates and not  recombinantly  and  that means for example if you're using a  collagenase and that still has a tryptic  or chemotryptic residual activities that  will cleave off your targets because the  collagenase itself is very specific  however a certain amount of these  activities  is also beneficial for in particular  task tissues and that's the reason why  we have this strategy that we use  combinations of enzymes that are each  very pure  so we know exactly which activities  specific unspecific specific activities  they have but then we use a combination  of these highly pure enzymes  to still get a good  dissociation and a high viability but  the advantage here is then now of course  because you have these mixture of highly  purified enzymes that you do not have  this lot to lot inconsistency so you do  not have to worry that one lot might be  great a sensitive epitope whereas the  next slot has a different of the  different behavior and that is really  the the key to do so  and how we check this is  we do  epitope stability tests on very  sensitive systems such as pvmc so we  take cell lines or primary cells blood  cells that express these surface markers  and then we incubate them with the  enzymes  and that is very um sensitive because  there's no extracellular matrix so the  enzymes can really act on the cell  surface proteins and by this you then by  flow cytometry we check if the mean  fluorescence intensity so the signal  strength is going down by the enzyme  incubation different titus  and by this we can really narrow down  what epitope sensitivities we are seeing  in terms of certain epitopes and certain  enzymes and can tightly control this and  by this  you can be sure that you do not have a  lot of false negatives which you would  have when you use either impure enzymes  or very aggressive proteins such as  trypsin or papain or displace or  something like that  hey thank you  next question are you planning on  applying this method to identify targets  and other types of tumors  yes yes so today i was talking about  pancreatic adenocarcinoma  but we are also applying the very same  method to other tumor indications  mostly of course solid tumor indications  because the workflow is really optimized  for solid tumor indications  and i mean our focus is really  immunotherapy so car targets  we are also working with  tcrs  that was not a focus of today because  they are the method of discoveries of  course very different one you're using  more  ngs  applications but still for functionality  testing the very same workflows can be  used as well as for example for  checkpoint inhibition and which is also  applied to multiple two medications  okay  it looks like we have time for one more  question  how did you select the initial antibody  screening panels  um yes so uh  basically we have um  we have a screening plate  it's uh it's it's a plate it's a um  it's a cell surface screening panel and  basically it contains  all the antibodies  that are expressed on cell surface we  have it  for human and um so it's a pre-selection  of let's say of all the portfolio  antibodies that are directed to work in  such essays  and um  this is also constantly updated so the  the portfolio is expanding and therefore  also these screening plates get  get larger  and  what we then sometimes add if they are  not anyway in these screening plates is  for example targets that are known from  other tumor indications and where  antibodies that are amendable for either  immune isochemistry or flow cytometry in  particular as i showed today  are available then we add them to the  place  thank you olaf do you have any final  comments for our audience  yeah i mean i would like uh to thank you  all for uh depending on your time zone  getting up very early or staying very  long  for  the presentation today and i hope um you  liked it and enjoyed it and yeah if any  question comes up in the in the future  please just get in touch with us thanks  a lot  thank you again olaf for your time today  in your presentation  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor miltoni biotech for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  until next time goodbye


